### Indexer, the one component that is not permissive

| Component | Version | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| `envio` | 3.10.0 | Commercial EULA, not an OSI-recognised open source licence |

`indexer/package.json` pins `envio` 3.10.0, whose `package.json` declares
`SEE LICENSE IN licenses/README.md`. That file states plainly that "Envio and its products do not use
a recognized open source license by the OSI", that self-hosting is permitted, then that "forking and
modifying code is not allowed". The operative grant in `licenses/LICENSE.md` reads:

> Company grants User a non-exclusive, non-transferable, limited license to access and use the SaaS
> for business or personal purposes in accordance with this Agreement.

and its restrictions forbid modifying, redistributing or reverse engineering it.

How that lines up with how it is used here: the indexer is run self-hosted against an RPC URL, which
is the use the licence permits. `envio` is not modified, not redistributed and not re-served. It is
installed from the registry at the pinned version. No envio code is compiled into the contracts or
bundled into the served app: it is a separate backfill process.

### Contracts

`contracts/lib/forge-std` is the only Foundry dependency, MIT or Apache-2.0, used by the test suites
only. It is gitignored and restored at the pinned revision by `scripts/install-forge-std.sh`. No
third-party code is compiled into the deployed bytecode. `contracts/NOTICE` carries the detail for
the Solidity side.

### Fonts

Inter, JetBrains Mono and Space Grotesk are pulled by `next/font/google` and self-hosted into the
build output, so they are served to every visitor. Their grants are recorded in `DATA-SOURCES.md`.

